{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01Qkdv6JJKQK7zweCtSRrJkj", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228687621_Ethnobotany_of_the_Yali\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yali_people\"}, {\"url\": \"https://factsanddetails.com/indonesia/Minorities_and_Regions/sub6_3j/entry-4037.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honai\"}, {\"url\": \"http://thespicerouteend.com/south-baliem-valley-from-wamena-to-angguruk/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yali_(politician)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://venturetheplanet.com/an-adventure-over-the-remote-papuan-mountain-jungles-into-the-rarely-visited-villages-of-the-last-penis-gourded-tribes-dani-lani-and-yali/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.indonesia-tourism.com/blog/the-endanger-and-beauty-yali/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/print/15968/ID\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:14:59.640Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Yali people pigs kept house \"pig\" honai Yalimo village", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_0138tpTUNwUrVVMdLKbEBgt7", "content": [{"title": "(PDF) Ethnobotany of the Yali", "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228687621_Ethnobotany_of_the_Yali"}, {"title": "Yali people", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yali_people"}, {"title": "DANI AND PEOPLE OF THE BALIEM VALLEY: HISTORY, RELIGION, WHERE THEY LIVE", "url": "https://factsanddetails.com/indonesia/Minorities_and_Regions/sub6_3j/entry-4037.html"}, {"title": "Honai", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honai"}, {"title": "South Baliem Valley : From Wamena to Angguruk", "url": "http://thespicerouteend.com/south-baliem-valley-from-wamena-to-angguruk/"}, {"title": "Yali (politician)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yali_(politician)"}, {"title": "An Adventure Over the Remote Papuan Mountain Jungles Into the Rarely Visited Villages of the Last Penis Gourded Tribes-Dani and Yali", "url": "https://venturetheplanet.com/an-adventure-over-the-remote-papuan-mountain-jungles-into-the-rarely-visited-villages-of-the-last-penis-gourded-tribes-dani-lani-and-yali/"}, {"title": "The Endanger And Beauty Yali", "url": "https://www.indonesia-tourism.com/blog/the-endanger-and-beauty-yali/"}, {"title": "Yali, Pass Valley in Indonesia Profile", "url": "https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/print/15968/ID"}]}, "The search turned up consistent evidence that Yali households keep pigs inside or beside their houses, though the sources differ on the exact arrangement.\n\n- **Pigs in the men's house:** A 1992 ethnobotany study of a Yali village says the men and older boys live in one house (yowi), where the pigs are also kept at night, while women and young children live in separate houses. The men and older boys inhabit one house (yowi), in which the pigs are also kept at night, and the women and young children live in others (homiya).\n- **Separate pig house:** A different summary describes a typical Yali village as having a men's house, a women's house, a wam ibam – pig house, and a sacred house for male rituals. It also says they live in smoky rectangular huts with pigs.\n- **Honai terminology:** Honai is primarily a Dani term. The Wikipedia entry says there are three types of honai: honai (for males), ebai (for females), and wamai (for animals). The Yali-specific term for the men's house in the ethnobotany source is yowi, so honai is likely a Dani word used loosely for Yali houses. A travel account also notes that regularly you see naked kids and pigs running out of the honai.\n- **Role of pigs:** Pigs carry major social weight. One source says the Yali also raise pigs, which have a central function in religious and communal life. Another notes that a man's wealth is measured by the number of pigs and wives he possesses.\n- **Caveats:** Much of the material is dated or secondhand. The ethnobotany fieldwork dates from 1992, and the Yali village descriptions on travel and general-reference sites are less rigorous. The Yali are also split into Yali (mo) in Yalimo Regency and Yali Mek in Yahukimo Regency, so practices may vary between groups."], "durationSeconds": 6.707464167, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01Qkdv6JJKQK7zweCtSRrJkj", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
